  "worked_solution": "Let $V$ be the event that the chosen pet is vaccinated, and let $D$ be the event that the pet is a dog. Then ${D}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the pet is a cat.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pet is vaccinated, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(V)=P(V|D)P(D)+P(V|{D}^{\u2032})P({D}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the pets in the center are dogs and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are cats, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P({D}^{\u2032})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the dogs are vaccinated, we have\n\n$$\nP(V|D)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{7}{15}$ of the pets are vaccinated, we have\n\n$$\nP(V)=\\frac{7}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(V|{D}^{\u2032})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V|D)P(D)+P(V|{D}^{\u2032})P({D}^{\u2032}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{15} & =(\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{3}{5})+P(V|{D}^{\u2032})(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{15} & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}P(V|{D}^{\u2032}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}P(V|{D}^{\u2032}) & =\\frac{7-3}{15} \\\\ P(V|{D}^{\u2032}) & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cat assisted in the center is vaccinated is $\\frac{2}{3}.$",
